messages:
akshay: Quick q — how should SDKs surface cancellation semantics for streaming/paginated endpoints? Specifically async iterators where transport retries might restart from a cursor. We have AbortSignal (TS), context.Context (Go), generator.close()/aclose() (Py). Should user cancel mean: (A) abort current RPC and stop all retries and end the iterator, or (B) signal transient cancel (stop current call) but allow SDK to auto-retry and resume from last acknowledged cursor? :thinking_face:

maria: My vote is A by default. If user explicitly cancels, they intended to stop. Auto-retries are surprising if they cause more items to be emitted after a cancel.

leo: Agreed on default A. But we need a clear opt-in for automatic resume/retry for resilient consumers. Maybe two orthogonal primitives: user cancel vs client auto-resume. Also we should add an explicit resume API so consumers can reattach.

jen: Implementation nuance: AbortSignal in TS is one-shot. For auto-reconnect we need the SDK to internally recreate signals. Proposal: expose two controls: `iter.cancel()` (user intent) and `ClientConfig.autoResumeOnTransient=true/false`. Docs should show both.

samir: On transport side we need to know why a call ended. Suggest transport layer pass back a `TerminationReason` + `cursor` when available. Also add a `shouldRetry(error, cancelled_flag)` hook so transport logic can suppress retries when cancelled by user.

akshay: Proposal sketch: default behavior = user cancel terminates iterator and prevents SDK retries. SDK will still retry on transport failures unless cancelled. Add `Iterator.resume(token)` and client config `auto_resume_on_recoverable_errors` (false by default). Also persist last seen cursor as `Iterator.last_cursor`.

leo: Language mapping: Go -> respect `ctx.Done()` as immediate user cancellation and set cancelled_flag. Python -> `async for`/`aclose()` should mark iterator cancelled; `await iterator.aclose()` will stop retries. TypeScript -> `AbortSignal` aborted sets cancelled_flag. Example in TS: 

```ts
const it = client.listEvents({pageSize: 200});
for await (const e of it) {
  if (shouldStop(e)) { it.cancel(); break; }
}
// can later: await client.resumeIterator(it.last_cursor);
```

jen: Edge: if iterator yields a resume token as last item and user cancels immediately after, we should persist token so resume works. Add `Iterator.last_cursor` and `Iterator.metadata` snapshot.

samir: I'll draft a tiny transport shim change: add `cancelled` flag to retry policy evaluation and expose `TerminationReason` enum (USER_CANCEL, RECOVERABLE_ERROR, FATAL). Will open PR tonight.

dev-bot: samir added samir to the thread

maria: Sounds good. Once Samir has PR we can update "SDK ergonomics: iteration & cancellation" doc and add quick examples per language. Thanks everyone :thumbsup:
